\transclude{GA1Y}

\p{\strong{Separability}}

\p{Over #{\mathbb{R}} or #{\mathbb{C}} we know from basic calculus that a polynomial #{f} has a repeated root #{\alpha} iff #{f(\alpha) = f'(\alpha) = 0}. To work over arbitrary fields we proceed purely algebraically (no calculus!).}

\transclude{GA1Z}

\p{\strong{Exercise.} Check this definition satisfies #{(f+g)' = f' + g'} and #{(fg)' = fg' + f'g}.}

\transclude{GA20}

\p{By the GCD of polynomials #{f, g \in K[X]}, not both #{0}, we mean the unique monic polynomial #{\gcd(f, g)} which generates the ideal #{(f, g) \subset K[X]}. This is the unique monic polynomial which divides #{f} and #{g} and can be written as #{af + bg} for some #{a, b \in K[X]}.}

\p{We can compute #{\gcd(f, g)}, together with #{a, b}, using Euclid’s algorithm.}

